Main points:

- `$` checks attributes, then items;
- `[` and `[[` check items, then attributes

 R does it like [this](https://github.com/yihui/knitr/blob/87789284d153f8ef36fe4f098a037d012af009db/R/block.R#L195-L204).  The rest of the engines [rely](https://github.com/yihui/knitr/blob/33a3ba65771e2ebc37a403cb37f941f2c6660bfb/R/engine.R#L81) on `knitr::engine_output()`.

`eng_python` is not using `engine_output()` yet, however even when it will, that won't help since it will [bypass the "echo" logic](https://github.com/yihui/knitr/blob/33a3ba65771e2ebc37a403cb37f941f2c6660bfb/R/engine.R#L63)

Currently, as a workaround I've defined
```r
eng_python <- function(opts) {
  out <- reticulate::eng_python(opts)
  if (opts$echo) {
    out
  } else if (length(out) > 1) {
    out[seq(2, length(out), 2)]
  }
}
```
in my Rmds, though it's not particularly pretty.

Some Python code I'm translating to R looks like this:

```python
def feature_columns_to_placeholders(feature_columns, batch_size=None):
  return {
    column.name: tf.placeholder(tf.float32, [batch_size])
    for column in feature_columns
  }
```

Note that the dictionary key `column.name` is actually evaluated and its result is used as the key, which is out-of-line with how R does things. For that reason, it would be useful to be able to construct a dictionary directly from an R list or environment, so we could write the roughly equivalent R code:

```R
placeholders <- lapply(feature_columns, function(column) {
  tf$placeholder(tf$float32, list(batch_size))
})
as.dict(placeholders)
```
